Lagos is a Portuguese city in the district of Faro and sub-region of the Algarve, located in the western Algarve. It is the seat of a municipality of 212.99 km² of area and 33,514 inhabitants (census of 2021), subdivided into 4 parishes. The municipality is bordered to the north by Monchique, to the east by Portimão, to the west by Vila do Bispo, to the northwest by Aljezur and to the south by the Atlantic Ocean.

Lagos is historically associated with Portuguese discoveries, being today one of the most attractive tourist centers in the Algarve, with beaches and an exceptional cultural and religious heritage, as well as a nightlife of great dynamism. 

It is known for its old walled town, cliffs and Atlantic beaches. A steep wooden walk leads to the sandy cove of Camilo Beach. The nearby cliffs of Ponta da Piedade offer stunning views of the promontory and a lighthouse. St. Anthony's Church, an ornate 18th-century church, is located in front of the Governors' Castle, a castle with a baroque façade and towers. 

Due to its unique coastline and natural and cultural beauty, Lagos is a city that relies heavily on tourist activity, but it is also a city that has not lost its identity, symbols, traditions and culture. This village is composed mainly of three-storey houses of light color, housing many shops, galleries and other hotel establishments. 

